Compare three rounds of AUTH CPU-time measurements. Interleave short blocks
and alternate their order, preserving 3*n requests per case and the original
30% regression limit. Fixed-order full blocks failed by 40.8% on Linux;
the same operation varied 2.7-fold between blocks, without matching GC cost.
Do not discard slow samples or retry until the timing assertion passes.

Generate one unrestricted TLS credential for each notification service test
and reuse it across connections and server restarts.  tls 2.4 rejects the
server-only fixture for client authentication, while generating a new
credential for every connection changes the SMP service identity.

On timing failure, report the SUB/SEND phase, CPU time, monotonic wall time
and optional +RTS -T GC statistics. timeit-2.0 measures process CPU time;
keep that assertion metric and its existing threshold unchanged.

Read service-subscription metrics strictly. The lazy reader leaves the
handle locked while the periodic writer reopens it; its resource-busy
exception terminates the server and makes the client fail with TLS EOF.

